# ── infrastructure: the secrets, and where each is consumed ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# .env.ci, .env.stage and .env.prod in this directory are committed and hold non-secret values. This
# file documents the variables that are NOT committed for stage/prod. They live in the GitHub
# Environments `STAGE` and `PROD` (used by .github/workflows/deploy-infrastructure.yml) and, because
# a Coolify Docker Compose resource interpolates the compose file with its own environment rather
# than with `--env-file`, they also have to be entered into the Coolify INFRASTRUCTURE resource and
# marked as secret there.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local (gitignored) to run the stage/prod topology locally:
#   docker compose --env-file tools/docker/env/infrastructure/.env.stage \
#     --env-file tools/docker/env/infrastructure/.env.local \
#     -f tools/docker/docker-compose-infrastructure.yaml config
#
# `ci` needs none of this: .env.ci carries the demo credentials.
#
# The testbed stack's application resource has its own list — ../testbed/.env.example. Four of the
# credentials below are needed by BOTH resources and are repeated there: PROCESSPUZZLE_DB_PASSWORD,
# MINIO_SERVICE_PASSWORD, PLATFORM_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET and COOLIFY_TOKEN.

# ── Deployment ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The full webhook URL Coolify prints on the resource's Webhook page, `?uuid=<resource-uuid>` and
# all. Taking the whole URL from a secret is what keeps .github/actions/coolify-deploy
# platform-agnostic. Consumed by: deploy-infrastructure.yml → coolify-deploy.
#
# Store it WITHOUT a trailing `&force=false`: coolify-deploy/action.yml appends its own `force`
# parameter, and a duplicate one is ambiguous.
COOLIFY_WEBHOOK=
# Coolify API token, `deploy` permission only.
COOLIFY_TOKEN=

# ── Credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keycloak's own database role. Consumed by: postgres (POSTGRES_PASSWORD) and keycloak
# (KC_DB_PASSWORD) — one role, so one value. `:?`-guarded: `up` fails without it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# The application role that 10-init-db.sh creates and that owns every stack's database. Consumed by:
# postgres (10-init-db.sh) and pgweb (PP_DB_PASSWORD, passed to `--pass`) here, and by each backend
# (SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD) in the application resource.
PROCESSPUZZLE_DB_PASSWORD=
# Keycloak's bootstrap admin, and the account keycloak-init authenticates with. Both `:?`-guarded.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USERNAME=
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# MinIO root. Consumed by: minio, and by init-minio.sh to create the service account below.
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
# The Spring service account init-minio.sh creates. Consumed by: minio here and by each backend
# (MINIO_SECRET_KEY) in the application resource.
MINIO_SERVICE_PASSWORD=
# The master-realm service account keycloak-init provisions, used by a backend to manage realms and
# users. Consumed by: keycloak-init here and by each backend (KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET). Without
# it the identity ports fall back to their no-ops and every other feature still works.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The customer realm sends first-login activation links through this authenticated relay. These
# values must also be entered in the Coolify infrastructure resource, because keycloak-init — not
# the Biz resource — owns Keycloak's realm configuration.
KEYCLOAK_CUSTOM_SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ── The non-secret infrastructure variables ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not listed here, because they are not secret: the image tag, the network name, the six
# PP_*_PUBLISH port maps, KEYCLOAK_DB_*, PGWEB_DATABASE, KC_HOSTNAME, KC_HOSTNAME_STRICT,
# KC_INTERNAL_URL, the CUSTOM_CLIENT_* redirect locations, KEYCLOAK_CUSTOM_SMTP_* relay settings
# (except its password), MINIO_ROOT_USER and MINIO_SERVICE_USER all live in the committed .env.stage
# / .env.prod. They still have to be entered into the Coolify resource, since it does not read those
# files either — the difference is only that git holds their values.
